    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Versioning.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Versioning misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Versioning. API versioning manages incompatible contract changes so existing consumers have a predictable migration path.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Versioning: API versioning manages incompatible contract changes so existing consumers have a predictable migration path.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: An API may expose `/v1/orders` and `/v2/orders` while clients migrate to the newer representation.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Versioning adds maintenance cost, so backward-compatible evolution is often preferable when it can be done cleanly.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nAn API may expose `/v1/orders` and `/v2/orders` while clients migrate to the newer representation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Versioning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an API may expose `/v1/orders` and `/v2/orders` while clients migrate to the newer representation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Versioning as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Versioning.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
