    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nVersioning is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Versioning adds maintenance cost, so backward-compatible evolution is often preferable when it can be done cleanly. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Versioning gives you: API versioning manages incompatible contract changes so existing consumers have a predictable migration path.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Versioning adds maintenance cost, so backward-compatible evolution is often preferable when it can be done cleanly.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: An API may expose `/v1/orders` and `/v2/orders` while clients migrate to the newer representation.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAn API may expose `/v1/orders` and `/v2/orders` while clients migrate to the newer representation.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Versioning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an API may expose `/v1/orders` and `/v2/orders` while clients migrate to the newer representation. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Versioning as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Versioning.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
