    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Pagination.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPagination divides a large result set into smaller responses using page numbers, offsets, or cursors.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Pagination accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Pagination divides a large result set into smaller responses using page numbers, offsets, or cursors.\n2. Use a concrete case: For a large result set, return a bounded page rather than every row; cursor-based pagination can be more stable than large offsets under frequent inserts.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Offset pagination is simple but can become unstable or expensive on changing large datasets; cursor-based approaches often handle those cases better.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Pagination first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a large result set, return a bounded page rather than every row; cursor-based pagination can be more stable than large offsets under frequent inserts.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Pagination when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a large result set, return a bounded page rather than every row; cursor-based pagination can be more stable than large offsets under frequent inserts. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Pagination as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Pagination.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Pagination, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
