    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Rate limiting is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Rate limiting controls how much traffic an identity, client, or resource may send over a period or according to a defined algorithm.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Rate limiting controls how much traffic an identity, client, or resource may send over a period or according to a defined algorithm.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Limits protect capacity and abuse resistance, but an overly strict policy can reject legitimate bursts.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nLimit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Rate limiting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Rate limiting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Rate limiting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
