    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Rate limiting.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Rate limiting comes from the behavior it provides: Rate limiting controls how much traffic an identity, client, or resource may send over a period or according to a defined algorithm. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Rate limiting controls how much traffic an identity, client, or resource may send over a period or according to a defined algorithm.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n3. State the trade-off: Limits protect capacity and abuse resistance, but an overly strict policy can reject legitimate bursts.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nLimit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Rate limiting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Rate limiting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Rate limiting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
