    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Rate limiting misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Rate limiting. Rate limiting controls how much traffic an identity, client, or resource may send over a period or according to a defined algorithm.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Rate limiting: Rate limiting controls how much traffic an identity, client, or resource may send over a period or according to a defined algorithm.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Limits protect capacity and abuse resistance, but an overly strict policy can reject legitimate bursts.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nLimit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Rate limiting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Rate limiting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Rate limiting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
