    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Rate limiting.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRate limiting is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Limits protect capacity and abuse resistance, but an overly strict policy can reject legitimate bursts. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Rate limiting gives you: Rate limiting controls how much traffic an identity, client, or resource may send over a period or according to a defined algorithm.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Limits protect capacity and abuse resistance, but an overly strict policy can reject legitimate bursts.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nLimit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Rate limiting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, limit requests per identity, client, or resource to protect capacity and prevent one caller from consuming an unfair share of the service. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Rate limiting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Rate limiting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
