    slug: "api-webservice-what-are-the-main-benefits-of-caching-164",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Caching.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Caching comes from the behavior it provides: API caching stores reusable responses or data so repeated requests can avoid unnecessary work or network/database access. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: API caching stores reusable responses or data so repeated requests can avoid unnecessary work or network/database access.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: A product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n3. State the trade-off: Caching improves latency and load but introduces freshness and invalidation concerns.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nA product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Caching when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a product-catalog service can cache frequently read reference data and define how entries expire or are invalidated when the source changes. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Caching data without an invalidation or expiry strategy.\n- Ignoring stale reads and cache stampedes.\n- Assuming a cache removes the need for a correct source of truth.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
