    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Webhooks.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA webhook is an HTTP callback sent by one system to another when a subscribed event occurs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Webhooks accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A webhook is an HTTP callback sent by one system to another when a subscribed event occurs.\n2. Use a concrete case: A payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Webhook delivery is commonly retried, so receivers should authenticate the sender and make processing idempotent.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Webhooks first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Webhooks when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Webhooks as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Webhooks.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Webhooks, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
