    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Webhooks is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. A webhook is an HTTP callback sent by one system to another when a subscribed event occurs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: A webhook is an HTTP callback sent by one system to another when a subscribed event occurs.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Webhook delivery is commonly retried, so receivers should authenticate the sender and make processing idempotent.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Webhooks when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Webhooks as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Webhooks.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
