    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about Webhooks.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Webhooks misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Webhooks. A webhook is an HTTP callback sent by one system to another when a subscribed event occurs.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Webhooks: A webhook is an HTTP callback sent by one system to another when a subscribed event occurs.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Webhook delivery is commonly retried, so receivers should authenticate the sender and make processing idempotent.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Webhooks when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Webhooks as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Webhooks.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
