    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Webhooks when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Webhooks.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: A webhook is an HTTP callback sent by one system to another when a subscribed event occurs.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Webhook delivery is commonly retried, so receivers should authenticate the sender and make processing idempotent.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Webhooks when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Webhooks as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Webhooks.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Webhooks when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Webhooks.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: A webhook is an HTTP callback sent by one system to another when a subscribed event occurs.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Webhook delivery is commonly retried, so receivers should authenticate the sender and make processing idempotent.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use Webhooks when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a payment provider can POST a signed `payment.succeeded` event to a merchant endpoint after processing a charge. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Webhooks as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Webhooks.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
