    slug: "api-webservice-what-are-the-common-mistakes-with-graphql-185",
    shortDescription: "Frequently asked API-WebService interview question about GraphQL.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with GraphQL are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: GraphQL is an API query language and execution model where clients request the fields they need against a typed schema.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating GraphQL as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with GraphQL.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: A mobile client can request a user’s name and the latest three orders in one GraphQL operation without defining a new server endpoint for that exact shape.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA mobile client can request a user’s name and the latest three orders in one GraphQL operation without defining a new server endpoint for that exact shape.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use GraphQL when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a mobile client can request a user’s name and the latest three orders in one GraphQL operation without defining a new server endpoint for that exact shape. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating GraphQL as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with GraphQL.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to GraphQL. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
