    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would use GraphQL when the problem matches the behavior it provides: GraphQL is an API query language and execution model where clients request the fields they need against a typed schema. A concrete application is A mobile client can request a user’s name and the latest three orders in one GraphQL operation without defining a new server endpoint for that exact shape.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you can translate the concept into an engineering decision rather than reciting its definition.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the requirement.\n2. Check that GraphQL provides the required behavior: GraphQL is an API query language and execution model where clients request the fields they need against a typed schema.\n3. Apply it in a concrete case: A mobile client can request a user’s name and the latest three orders in one GraphQL operation without defining a new server endpoint for that exact shape.\n4. Reassess the choice if the workload or constraints change: Flexible queries can reduce over-fetching but require careful authorization, complexity limits, caching, and resolver performance controls.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nStart with the requirement, then explain why GraphQL fits it. I would give the concrete example and mention the main limitation so the answer shows judgment, not just tool familiarity.\n\n**Example :**\nA mobile client can request a user’s name and the latest three orders in one GraphQL operation without defining a new server endpoint for that exact shape.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use GraphQL when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a mobile client can request a user’s name and the latest three orders in one GraphQL operation without defining a new server endpoint for that exact shape. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating GraphQL as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with GraphQL.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDescribe the requirement that led you to choose it. “I would use it because it is popular” is much weaker than explaining the actual problem.",
