    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about gRPC when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to gRPC.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: gRPC is a high-performance RPC framework commonly using Protocol Buffers and HTTP/2 for strongly typed service communication.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: An internal service can define a typed RPC contract in a `.proto` file and generate client/server code in supported languages.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: gRPC is well suited to internal service-to-service communication, while browsers and public APIs may favor HTTP/JSON depending on requirements.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nAn internal service can define a typed RPC contract in a `.proto` file and generate client/server code in supported languages.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn http api or service integration may use gRPC when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an internal service can define a typed RPC contract in a `.proto` file and generate client/server code in supported languages. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating gRPC as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with gRPC.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
