    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Algorithms when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Algorithms.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: An algorithm is a finite procedure for transforming inputs into a desired result. The useful part of an algorithm answer is its correctness and complexity.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A search service can use an efficient algorithm to avoid scanning every record when the data set becomes large.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Algorithms, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA search service can use an efficient algorithm to avoid scanning every record when the data set becomes large.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Algorithms when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a search service can use an efficient algorithm to avoid scanning every record when the data set becomes large. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Algorithms as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Algorithms.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
