    shortDescription: "Frequently asked ComputerScience interview question about Big O.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nBig O describes an asymptotic upper-growth bound for resource usage as input size increases.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Big O accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Big O describes an asymptotic upper-growth bound for resource usage as input size increases.\n2. Use a concrete case: Use Big O to explain why an O(n log n) approach scales better than an O(n²) approach for a large input.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Big O, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Big O first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nUse Big O to explain why an O(n log n) approach scales better than an O(n²) approach for a large input.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Big O when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use Big O to explain why an O(n log n) approach scales better than an O(n²) approach for a large input. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Big O as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Big O.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Big O, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
