    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Big O when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Big O.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Big O describes an asymptotic upper-growth bound for resource usage as input size increases.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Use Big O to explain why an O(n log n) approach scales better than an O(n²) approach for a large input.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For Big O, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nUse Big O to explain why an O(n log n) approach scales better than an O(n²) approach for a large input.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Big O when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use Big O to explain why an O(n log n) approach scales better than an O(n²) approach for a large input. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Big O as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Big O.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
