    shortDescription: "Frequently asked ComputerScience interview question about Recursion.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Recursion in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: Traverse a tree by handling the current node and then recursively visiting its child subtrees, with a clear base case.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Recursion solves a problem by having a function call itself on smaller or simpler inputs with a base case.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: Traverse a tree by handling the current node and then recursively visiting its child subtrees, with a clear base case.\n4. Finish with the limitation: For Recursion, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nTraverse a tree by handling the current node and then recursively visiting its child subtrees, with a clear base case.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Recursion when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, traverse a tree by handling the current node and then recursively visiting its child subtrees, with a clear base case. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Recursion as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Recursion.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
