    shortDescription: "Frequently asked ComputerScience interview question about Concurrency.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because concurrency means multiple units of work can make progress during overlapping periods, even if they do not execute simultaneously. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: coordinate two concurrent updates to the same account so a lost update cannot silently overwrite another request.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Concurrency addresses it: Concurrency means multiple units of work can make progress during overlapping periods, even if they do not execute simultaneously.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Coordinate two concurrent updates to the same account so a lost update cannot silently overwrite another request.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Concurrency or distribution can improve throughput and resilience, but it introduces coordination, ordering, failure, and debugging concerns.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nCoordinate two concurrent updates to the same account so a lost update cannot silently overwrite another request.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Concurrency when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, coordinate two concurrent updates to the same account so a lost update cannot silently overwrite another request. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Choosing a concurrency mechanism without identifying the shared state.\n- Ignoring deadlocks, contention, or lost updates.\n- Assuming a stronger isolation level is always the best answer.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
