    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nACID describes transaction properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define ACID accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: ACID describes transaction properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.\n2. Use a concrete case: Wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For ACID, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define ACID first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nWrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use ACID when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating ACID as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with ACID.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor ACID, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
