    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because aCID describes transaction properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how ACID addresses it: ACID describes transaction properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For ACID, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nWrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use ACID when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating ACID as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with ACID.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because aCID describes transaction properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how ACID addresses it: ACID describes transaction properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For ACID, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nWrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use ACID when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating ACID as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with ACID.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
