    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for ACID is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. ACID describes transaction properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: ACID describes transaction properties: atomicity, consistency, isolation, and durability.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For ACID, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nWrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use ACID when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, wrap related order and payment-record changes in a database transaction when they must commit or roll back together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating ACID as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with ACID.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
