    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nNormalization organizes relational data to reduce unnecessary duplication and update anomalies.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Normalization accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Normalization organizes relational data to reduce unnecessary duplication and update anomalies.\n2. Use a concrete case: Store customer details once and reference them from orders instead of duplicating the same customer data in every row.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Normalization, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Normalization first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nStore customer details once and reference them from orders instead of duplicating the same customer data in every row.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Normalization when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, store customer details once and reference them from orders instead of duplicating the same customer data in every row. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Normalization, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
