    shortDescription: "Frequently asked ComputerScience interview question about Normalization.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Normalization comes from the behavior it provides: Normalization organizes relational data to reduce unnecessary duplication and update anomalies. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Store customer details once and reference them from orders instead of duplicating the same customer data in every row.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Normalization organizes relational data to reduce unnecessary duplication and update anomalies.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Store customer details once and reference them from orders instead of duplicating the same customer data in every row.\n3. State the trade-off: For Normalization, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nStore customer details once and reference them from orders instead of duplicating the same customer data in every row.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Normalization when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, store customer details once and reference them from orders instead of duplicating the same customer data in every row. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
