    shortDescription: "Frequently asked ComputerScience interview question about Indexes.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Indexes comes from the behavior it provides: Indexes provide additional structures that can speed selected queries at the cost of storage and write work. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: An index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Indexes provide additional structures that can speed selected queries at the cost of storage and write work.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: An index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n3. State the trade-off: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nAn index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Indexes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Indexes comes from the behavior it provides: Indexes provide additional structures that can speed selected queries at the cost of storage and write work. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: An index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Indexes provide additional structures that can speed selected queries at the cost of storage and write work.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: An index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n3. State the trade-off: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nAn index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Indexes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
