    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Indexes in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: An index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Indexes provide additional structures that can speed selected queries at the cost of storage and write work.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: An index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Faster reads can come with extra storage, write overhead, or plan complexity, so the access pattern should justify the change.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nAn index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use Indexes when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an index on `orders(customer_id, created_at)` can support a query that retrieves recent orders for one customer, but the query plan should be checked before and after adding it. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
