    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of OSI comes from the behavior it provides: The OSI model separates network communication into conceptual layers such as physical, data link, network, transport, and application. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: For example, in a software system, an engineer may run into this concept when the osi model separates network communication into conceptual layers such as physical, data link, network, transport, and application. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: The OSI model separates network communication into conceptual layers such as physical, data link, network, transport, and application.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: For example, in a software system, an engineer may run into this concept when the osi model separates network communication into conceptual layers such as physical, data link, network, transport, and application. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n3. State the trade-off: For OSI, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nFor example, in a software system, an engineer may run into this concept when the osi model separates network communication into conceptual layers such as physical, data link, network, transport, and application. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use OSI when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for example, in a software system, an engineer may run into this concept when the osi model separates network communication into conceptual layers such as physical, data link, network, transport, and application. The implementation can then be checked against the expected behavior. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating OSI as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with OSI.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
