    shortDescription: "Frequently asked ComputerScience interview question about TCP UDP.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of TCP UDP comes from the behavior it provides: TCP provides connection-oriented, reliable byte-stream delivery; UDP provides datagram delivery with fewer protocol-level guarantees. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Use TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: TCP provides connection-oriented, reliable byte-stream delivery; UDP provides datagram delivery with fewer protocol-level guarantees.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Use TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable.\n3. State the trade-off: For TCP UDP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nUse TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use TCP UDP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating TCP UDP as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with TCP UDP.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
