    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen TCP UDP misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to TCP UDP. TCP provides connection-oriented, reliable byte-stream delivery; UDP provides datagram delivery with fewer protocol-level guarantees.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls TCP UDP: TCP provides connection-oriented, reliable byte-stream delivery; UDP provides datagram delivery with fewer protocol-level guarantees.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Use TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: For TCP UDP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nUse TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use TCP UDP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating TCP UDP as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with TCP UDP.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
