    shortDescription: "Frequently asked ComputerScience interview question about TCP UDP.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about TCP UDP when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to TCP UDP.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: TCP provides connection-oriented, reliable byte-stream delivery; UDP provides datagram delivery with fewer protocol-level guarantees.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Use TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: For TCP UDP, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nUse TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA software system may use TCP UDP when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use TCP for ordered reliable delivery such as file transfer, while a latency-sensitive stream may choose UDP when some loss is acceptable. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating TCP UDP as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with TCP UDP.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
