    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nArrays is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. For Arrays, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Arrays gives you: An array stores elements in indexed positions, usually with efficient direct access by index.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: For Arrays, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Store indexed values such as sensor readings in an array when direct positional access matters.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nStore indexed values such as sensor readings in an array when direct positional access matters.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nApplication code that processes data may use Arrays when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, store indexed values such as sensor readings in an array when direct positional access matters. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Arrays as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Arrays.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
