    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA hash table maps keys to values using a hash function and buckets or another collision-handling structure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Hash tables accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: A hash table maps keys to values using a hash function and buckets or another collision-handling structure.\n2. Use a concrete case: Map a user ID to its record so a lookup does not require scanning every user.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For Hash tables, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Hash tables first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nMap a user ID to its record so a lookup does not require scanning every user.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nApplication code that processes data may use Hash tables when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, map a user ID to its record so a lookup does not require scanning every user. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Hash tables as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Hash tables.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Hash tables, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
