    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for BFS is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Breadth-first search explores vertices level by level, typically using a queue.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Breadth-first search explores vertices level by level, typically using a queue.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Use breadth-first search to find a shortest path in an unweighted graph.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: For BFS, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nUse breadth-first search to find a shortest path in an unweighted graph.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nApplication code that processes data may use BFS when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use breadth-first search to find a shortest path in an unweighted graph. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating BFS as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with BFS.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
