    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Sorting comes from the behavior it provides: Sorting rearranges elements according to an ordering relation. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Sort events by timestamp before merging or scanning them in chronological order.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: Sorting rearranges elements according to an ordering relation.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Sort events by timestamp before merging or scanning them in chronological order.\n3. State the trade-off: For Sorting, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nSort events by timestamp before merging or scanning them in chronological order.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nApplication code that processes data may use Sorting when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, sort events by timestamp before merging or scanning them in chronological order. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Sorting as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Sorting.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
