    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because dynamic programming solves problems by defining reusable subproblems and combining their results. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: cache overlapping subproblem results, such as minimum cost to reach each position, instead of recomputing them.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Dynamic programming addresses it: Dynamic programming solves problems by defining reusable subproblems and combining their results.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Cache overlapping subproblem results, such as minimum cost to reach each position, instead of recomputing them.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: For Dynamic programming, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nCache overlapping subproblem results, such as minimum cost to reach each position, instead of recomputing them.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nApplication code that processes data may use Dynamic programming when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, cache overlapping subproblem results, such as minimum cost to reach each position, instead of recomputing them. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Dynamic programming as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Dynamic programming.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
