    shortDescription: "Frequently asked CyberSeqcurity interview question about Security fundamentals.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSecurity protects systems and data from unauthorized access, misuse, disruption, or disclosure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Security fundamentals accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Security protects systems and data from unauthorized access, misuse, disruption, or disclosure.\n2. Use a concrete case: For a file-upload endpoint, identify trusted and untrusted boundaries, validate inputs, and restrict stored files so uploaded data cannot execute as code.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Security fundamentals first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a file-upload endpoint, identify trusted and untrusted boundaries, validate inputs, and restrict stored files so uploaded data cannot execute as code.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use Security fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a file-upload endpoint, identify trusted and untrusted boundaries, validate inputs, and restrict stored files so uploaded data cannot execute as code. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Security fundamentals, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
