    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Security fundamentals is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Security protects systems and data from unauthorized access, misuse, disruption, or disclosure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Security protects systems and data from unauthorized access, misuse, disruption, or disclosure.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: For a file-upload endpoint, identify trusted and untrusted boundaries, validate inputs, and restrict stored files so uploaded data cannot execute as code.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a file-upload endpoint, identify trusted and untrusted boundaries, validate inputs, and restrict stored files so uploaded data cannot execute as code.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use Security fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a file-upload endpoint, identify trusted and untrusted boundaries, validate inputs, and restrict stored files so uploaded data cannot execute as code. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
