    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSecurity fundamentals is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Security fundamentals gives you: Security protects systems and data from unauthorized access, misuse, disruption, or disclosure.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Stronger controls reduce risk but can add latency, operational work, or user friction; the control should match the threat and resource being protected.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: For a file-upload endpoint, identify trusted and untrusted boundaries, validate inputs, and restrict stored files so uploaded data cannot execute as code.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a file-upload endpoint, identify trusted and untrusted boundaries, validate inputs, and restrict stored files so uploaded data cannot execute as code.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use Security fundamentals when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a file-upload endpoint, identify trusted and untrusted boundaries, validate inputs, and restrict stored files so uploaded data cannot execute as code. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating internal location as proof of trust.\n- Logging secrets or sensitive data while trying to improve diagnosis.\n- Checking authentication but forgetting authorization at the resource boundary.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
