    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe CIA triad describes confidentiality, integrity, and availability as core security properties.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define CIA triad accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: The CIA triad describes confidentiality, integrity, and availability as core security properties.\n2. Use a concrete case: Protect a customer record by controlling access for confidentiality, validating writes for integrity, and designing recovery for availability.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: For CIA triad, the useful benefit has to be weighed against the extra complexity, maintenance, or cost introduced by the approach.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define CIA triad first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nProtect a customer record by controlling access for confidentiality, validating writes for integrity, and designing recovery for availability.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA security-sensitive application may use CIA triad when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, protect a customer record by controlling access for confidentiality, validating writes for integrity, and designing recovery for availability. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CIA triad as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CIA triad.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor CIA triad, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
